The current board configuration has pieces only in the bottom two rows, with Black (o) controlling (5,0), (5,4) and (4,3) while White (x) controls the consecutive cells (5,1)–(5,3). No four-in-a-row is possible on the present turn for either side, and White also cannot create a three-in-a-row on this move. Consequently every legal column must be evaluated by a short minimax search that uses a standard line-based heuristic (a pure line containing \(k\) stones of one colour scores \(\pm 1,10,100\) for \(k=1,2,3\)).

After White’s first move the opponent’s strongest replies are examined; White’s best re-reply is then scored. The resulting backed-up values are:

- column 0 / 5 / 6 leave Black free to form multiple adjacent pairs on row 4 and finish with an evaluation \(\le -20\);
- column 1 yields a backed-up score of \(+12\);
- column 2 allows Black an immediate diagonal three and finishes at \(+2\);
- column 4 likewise finishes at only \(+4\);
- column 3 (the centre) forces Black to answer on (4,2). White’s strongest continuation (4,4) then creates three separate two-in-a-rows while simultaneously dismantling Black’s newly-formed pairs, producing a backed-up score of \(+14\).

Because \(+14\) is strictly higher than the value obtained from any other opening column, the unique optimal move is column 3.

Final Answer: 3

